---
// Projects.astro

const projects = [
  {
    index:   '01',
    status:  'Engineering Case Study',
    title:   'Urban Mobility Simulation Platform',
    tagline: 'GIS · Geospatial Analysis · Predictive Modeling',
    tags:    ['GeoDjango', 'PostGIS', 'K-Means', 'Shapely', 'Django Channels', 'Celery', 'React Native', 'Docker'],

    context:
      'City planning teams across West Africa lacked tooling to model vehicle distribution, predict demand hotspots, and simulate depot placement decisions at metropolitan scale. Decisions were made on static spreadsheets with no spatial reasoning.',

    challenge:
      'The core engineering challenge was fusing heterogeneous geospatial data sources (OSM road networks, GPS telemetry, census polygons) into a unified query layer capable of running real-time spatial clustering without full table scans on datasets exceeding 500 K points.',

    architecture:
      'Django + GeoDjango backend with a PostGIS-powered spatial API. GPS telemetry is ingested via a Celery pipeline that batches writes and runs ST_ClusterKMeans clustering server-side. Demand zones are materialized as PostGIS polygon layers (convex hulls + concave polygons via ST_ConcaveHull). A WebSocket channel broadcasts cluster diffs to connected React Native dashboards.',

    decisions: [
      { decision: 'PostGIS over application-layer clustering', rationale: 'Push spatial computation to the database engine — eliminates Python serialization overhead and leverages GIST indexes for sub-50ms polygon intersection queries.' },
      { decision: 'Materialized views for zone snapshots', rationale: 'Cluster outputs are expensive. Materializing every N minutes and serving stale-but-fast reads for dashboards cut API response time from 1.8 s to 38 ms.' },
      { decision: 'Delta-only WebSocket payloads', rationale: 'Broadcasting full cluster state on each update at 500 K points is prohibitive. Structural diffs (add/remove/move zone) reduce WS payload size by ~97%.' },
    ],

    scalability:
      'Architecture supports horizontal Celery worker scaling. PostGIS spatial indexes maintain query performance at 10× current data volume. Redis pub/sub fan-out decouples ingestion throughput from connected client count.',

    metrics: [
      { label: 'Spatial query p95', value: '< 45ms'  },
      { label: 'Points ingested',   value: '500 K+'  },
      { label: 'WS payload delta',  value: '−97%'    },
    ],
    link: '#',
  },

  {
    index:   '02',
    status:  'Engineering Case Study',
    title:   'Real-Time Delivery Application',
    tagline: 'WebSockets · Live Tracking · Background Tasks',
    tags:    ['Django Channels', 'WebSockets', 'Redis', 'Celery', 'PostGIS', 'Expo', 'React Native'],

    context:
      'A last-mile logistics operator had zero real-time visibility into driver positions. Dispatchers relied on phone calls to track deliveries. The existing polling solution imposed a 20–35 s position lag and accounted for 60% of peak database load.',

    challenge:
      'Replace a polling architecture without downtime while maintaining compatibility with existing REST consumers. The synchronization model needed to handle intermittent mobile connectivity gracefully — GPS traces must never be lost, and the UI must reconcile out-of-order updates arriving from reconnected drivers.',

    architecture:
      'Django Channels layer with Redis as the channel layer backend. Driver apps (Expo/React Native) maintain a persistent WebSocket connection and publish GPS frames at 2 s intervals. The server validates, persists (async write via Celery), and fans out position deltas only to the authorized dispatcher room. ETA recalculation runs as a Celery periodic task reading the latest driver positions from a Redis hash (O(1) lookups). PostGIS stores full trip traces for analytics.',

    decisions: [
      { decision: 'Redis hash for live positions', rationale: 'A single HSET per driver update gives O(1) write and read. Querying current positions for 500 concurrent drivers takes ~0.3 ms, versus 80 ms with a DB round trip.' },
      { decision: 'Celery for trace persistence', rationale: 'Decoupling WS handler from DB writes reduces handler latency from ~25 ms to ~1 ms and prevents DB pressure from cascading into WebSocket drops under load.' },
      { decision: 'Sequence numbers for reconciliation', rationale: 'Each GPS frame carries a monotonic seq. The React Native client buffers frames and reorders before applying to the map, eliminating visual jitter from out-of-order delivery.' },
    ],

    scalability:
      'Redis pub/sub fan-out means adding dispatchers does not increase driver-side write cost. Celery workers scale independently of the ASGI process pool. PostGIS partitioning by date keeps trace history queries fast as data grows.',

    metrics: [
      { label: 'Position lag',         value: '< 2 s'   },
      { label: 'Concurrent drivers',   value: '500+'    },
      { label: 'DB query reduction',   value: '−93%'    },
    ],
    link: '#',
  },

  {
    index:   '03',
    status:  'Engineering Case Study',
    title:   'Inventory Management SaaS',
    tagline: 'Scalable Backend · Invoicing Logic · System Architecture',
    tags:    ['Django', 'DRF', 'Multi-tenant', 'Celery Beat', 'PostgreSQL', 'Soft-deletes', 'React Native'],

    context:
      'SME retail operators across 3 countries managed stock across multiple branches using disconnected spreadsheets. Overselling, phantom inventory, and 3-day manual reconciliation cycles were standard. No audit trail existed for financial compliance.',

    challenge:
      'Design a multi-tenant data model where branch isolation, soft-delete semantics, and double-entry ledger logic must coexist without cross-tenant data leakage, while keeping query complexity manageable for a small engineering team to maintain.',

    architecture:
      'Row-level multi-tenancy via a TenantMiddleware that injects a tenant FK into every queryset through a custom Manager. Stock movements use a double-entry ledger pattern: every adjustment produces two StockEntry rows (debit + credit), making the audit log immutable. Soft-deletes are implemented via an is_deleted + deleted_at field with a custom QuerySet.delete() override and a SafeDeleteManager. Invoices are generated server-side via a Celery task (WeasyPrint + Jinja2) and queued for delivery via Celery Beat. React Native lets branch managers scan barcodes for real-time stock adjustments.',

    decisions: [
      { decision: 'Double-entry ledger over mutable stock fields', rationale: 'A mutable quantity field cannot be audited — any UPDATE destroys history. An append-only ledger makes every state recoverable and enables point-in-time inventory snapshots via SUM aggregation.' },
      { decision: 'Soft-deletes via SafeDeleteManager', rationale: 'Hard deletes break FK integrity and destroy audit context. Overriding delete() at the Manager level means all ORM operations are safe by default without per-view defensive checks.' },
      { decision: 'Celery Beat for invoice scheduling', rationale: 'Synchronous invoice generation blocks the request thread for 800 ms–1.5 s under load. Async generation + polling endpoint eliminates this bottleneck and enables retry logic on PDF failures.' },
    ],

    scalability:
      'Tenant-scoped queries leverage PostgreSQL partial indexes on (tenant_id, is_deleted). Ledger aggregation queries use materialized monthly snapshots as a base — full recalculation only runs from the last snapshot date. New tenants are provisioned in < 200 ms via a signal-driven setup flow.',

    metrics: [
      { label: 'Invoice generation', value: '< 1.2 s'      },
      { label: 'Tenant isolation',   value: 'Row-level'    },
      { label: 'Audit coverage',     value: '100% events'  },
    ],
    link: '#',
  },
  {
    index:    '04',
    status:   'Engineering Case Study',
    title:    'AgriMarket-Predictor',
    tagline:  'Predictive Modeling · Data Pipelines · Time-Series Forecasting',
    tags:     ['Django', 'Python', 'Scikit-Learn', 'Pandas', 'NumPy', 'Chart.js', 'PostgreSQL', 'Docker'],

    context:
      'Agricultural stakeholders and local traders often struggle with volatile market conditions, lacking data-driven tools to anticipate crop price fluctuations. Decisions on when to sell or store produce were based on intuition rather than historical price trends.',

    challenge:
      'The core engineering challenge was designing a clean, automated pipeline to ingest, clean, and structure historical market datasets, and then exposing time-series predictive models (like Regression/RandomForest) through a synchronous web API without blocking the Django request-response cycle.',

    architecture:
      'A modular Django architecture where data ingestion and preprocessing are decoupled from the visualization layer. Pandas and NumPy handle data cleaning and feature engineering (seasonality, moving averages) in isolation. The scikit-learn models are trained and persisted server-side, allowing a lightweight Django REST endpoint to serve live predictions. The frontend dynamically renders interactive price forecast trends using Chart.js.',

    decisions: [
      { decision: 'Server-side model persistence', rationale: 'Training models on the fly during an HTTP request is highly inefficient. Persisting trained models allows sub-10ms inference times when users query specific crop forecasts.' },
      { decision: 'Pandas for encapsulated data transformation', rationale: 'Moving data manipulation out of raw SQL queries and into a dedicated Python pandas pipeline ensures data consistency and makes it easy to add new exogenous features like historical rainfall profiles.' },
      { decision: 'Chart.js for client-side asynchronous rendering', rationale: 'Instead of generating heavy static charts on the backend, serving lightweight structured JSON prediction arrays shifts the rendering load to the client, maximizing server throughput.' },
    ],

    scalability:
      'The prediction engine is designed to be easily moved into an asynchronous pipeline or a microservice wrapper if data volume scales. Database structures scale linearly, leveraging indexing on commodity types and historical dates to maintain fast lookup times.',

    metrics: [
      { label: 'Inference latency',  value: '< 15ms'      },
      { label: 'Pipeline accuracy',  value: 'Optimized'   },
      { label: 'Frontend overhead',  value: 'Minimal'     },
    ],
    link: 'https://github.com/mimbang/AgriMarket-Predictor',
  },
  {
    index:    '05',
    status:   'Engineering Case Study',
    title:    'Wallet-Lite',
    tagline:  'Financial Ledger · Transaction Atomicity · Concurrent Writes',
    tags:     ['Django', 'DRF', 'PostgreSQL', 'Database Locking', 'REST API', 'Unit Testing', 'Docker'],

    context:
      'Digital wallet architectures require bulletproof reliability. Standard database setups fail when handling rapid, concurrent balance adjustments—such as simultaneous deposits and transfers—leading to inconsistent states, ghost balances, or double-spending.',

    challenge:
      'The core engineering challenge was guaranteeing strict transaction atomicity and preventing race conditions during concurrent wallet balances updates, ensuring that every financial debit and credit operation maintains 100% mathematical integrity without locking the entire database.',

    architecture:
      'A robust financial backend built with Django REST Framework and PostgreSQL. The application utilizes a localized locking mechanism to isolate account modifications. Balance operations are encapsulated inside atomic blocks, ensuring that if any step of a transfer fails, the entire state safely rolls back. The architecture features clean validation layers to catch negative balances or invalid state transitions before hitting the persistence layer.',

    decisions: [
      { decision: 'Row-level database locking (select_for_update)', rationale: 'Using Django’s .select_for_update() blocks concurrent reads/writes on that specific wallet row until the transaction commits, preventing race conditions and double-spending under high load.' },
      { decision: 'Atomic transaction boundaries', rationale: 'Wrapping the entire transfer pipeline in transaction.atomic() guarantees all-or-nothing execution. If a debit succeeds but the credit fails, the database automatically reverts, avoiding catastrophic data mismatches.' },
      { decision: 'Strict constraints at the model layer', rationale: 'Implementing database-level CheckConstraints on balances prevents an account from ever dropping below zero, acting as an immutable final line of defense against logic bypasses.' },
    ],

    scalability:
      'By avoiding global or table-level locks and focusing exclusively on row-level operations, the system scales horizontally alongside user growth. Read-heavy actions (like fetching balance history) run independently without being blocked by active ledger writes.',

    metrics: [
      { label: 'Data integrity',     value: '100%'        },
      { label: 'Race condition rate', value: '0%'         },
      { label: 'Query safety',       value: 'Row-isolated'},
    ],
    link: 'https://github.com/mimbang/Wallet-Lite',
  },
  {
index:    '02',
status:   'Engineering Case Study',
title:    'TrackFleet',
tagline:  'Realtime Delivery · Live Tracking · Event Synchronization',
tags:     ['Django', 'Django Channels', 'Redis', 'WebSockets', 'React Native', 'Expo'],

context:
'Real-time delivery systems require continuous synchronization between drivers, customers, and dispatch services. Standard polling-based architectures quickly become inefficient under high-frequency location updates and concurrent active sessions.',

challenge:
'The core challenge was designing a low-latency synchronization architecture capable of handling live delivery tracking, driver state updates, and real-time notifications while minimizing unnecessary network overhead.',

architecture:
'The backend uses Django Channels combined with Redis Pub/Sub for realtime event broadcasting. Mobile clients built with React Native and Expo maintain persistent WebSocket connections to receive delivery state changes instantly. Background workers process asynchronous notifications and delivery events independently from the main API.',

decisions: [
{
decision: 'WebSocket-first communication model',
rationale: 'Persistent bidirectional connections eliminate excessive polling and drastically improve realtime synchronization efficiency.'
},
{
decision: 'Redis Pub/Sub event distribution',
rationale: 'Redis enables lightweight event propagation between services while maintaining low message latency.'
},
{
decision: 'Decoupled notification workers',
rationale: 'Separating notifications from core delivery logic prevents secondary tasks from slowing realtime operations.'
},
],

scalability:
'The event-driven architecture allows independent scaling of realtime communication layers, worker nodes, and REST services depending on traffic patterns.',

metrics: [
{ label: 'Realtime transport', value: 'WebSockets' },
{ label: 'Event broker', value: 'Redis Pub/Sub' },
{ label: 'Synchronization model', value: 'Event-driven' },
],

link: 'https://github.com/',
},
];
---

<section id="projects" class="py-28">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">

    <!-- Section header -->
    <div class="flex items-end justify-between pb-7 mb-12 border-b border-[#161D30]">
      <div>
        <p class="label mb-2.5">// 03 — Flagship Work</p>
        <h2 class="font-sans font-semibold text-[1.65rem] text-[#E6EBF4] tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Engineering Case Studies
        </h2>
      </div>
      <span class="font-mono text-[0.62rem] text-[#3D4E6B] tracking-[0.08em] hidden sm:block">
        {projects.length} projects
      </span>
    </div>

    <!-- Projects -->
    <div class="space-y-5">
      {projects.map((project) => (
        <article class="group bg-[#0D1120] border border-[#161D30] hover:border-[rgba(16,185,129,0.2)] rounded-md overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300 shadow-[0_2px_16px_rgba(0,0,0,0.35),0_1px_0_rgba(255,255,255,0.03)]">

          <!-- ── Project title bar ───────────────────── -->
          <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center justify-between gap-3 px-6 py-4 border-b border-[#161D30]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
              <span class="font-mono text-[0.6rem] text-[#10B981]/50 tracking-[0.18em]">[{project.index}]</span>
              <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-[#E6EBF4] text-base tracking-[-0.015em]">
                {project.title}
              </h3>
              <span class="font-mono text-[0.62rem] text-[#3D4E6B] hidden sm:inline">
                — {project.tagline}
              </span>
            </div>
            <a
              href={project.link}
              class="group/link shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 font-mono text-[0.62rem] text-[#3D4E6B] hover:text-[#10B981] tracking-[0.1em] uppercase transition-colors duration-150"
            >
              View
              <svg class="w-3 h-3 group-hover/link:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform duration-150" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M13.5 4.5L21 12m0 0l-7.5 7.5M21 12H3"/>
              </svg>
            </a>
          </div>

          <!-- ── Case study body ─────────────────────── -->
          <div class="divide-y divide-[#161D30]">

            <!-- Row 1: Context + Challenge side by side -->
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 divide-y md:divide-y-0 md:divide-x divide-[#161D30]">
              <div class="px-6 py-5">
                <p class="label mb-3">Context</p>
                <p class="font-sans text-[#5A6A88] text-[0.8rem] leading-[1.7] font-light">
                  {project.context}
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-6 py-5">
                <p class="label mb-3">Technical Challenge</p>
                <p class="font-sans text-[#5A6A88] text-[0.8rem] leading-[1.7] font-light">
                  {project.challenge}
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Row 2: Architecture Overview (full width) -->
            <div class="px-6 py-5">
              <p class="label mb-3">Architecture Overview</p>
              <p class="font-sans text-[#7888A8] text-[0.82rem] leading-[1.75] font-light">
                {project.architecture}
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Row 3: Engineering Decisions -->
            <div class="px-6 py-5">
              <p class="label mb-4">Engineering Decisions</p>
              <div class="space-y-3.5">
                {project.decisions.map((d, i) => (
                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-5 gap-y-1 items-start">
                    <!-- Decision label -->
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 sm:pt-0.5">
                      <span class="font-mono text-[0.58rem] text-[#10B981]/40">{String(i + 1).padStart(2,'0')}.</span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-[0.68rem] text-[#10B981]/80 whitespace-nowrap">
                        {d.decision}
                      </span>
                    </div>
                    <!-- Rationale -->
                    <p class="font-sans text-[#5A6A88] text-[0.78rem] leading-[1.65] font-light pl-4 sm:pl-0 border-l border-[#1C2640] sm:border-l-0 sm:border-l-0">
                      {d.rationale}
                    </p>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Row 4: Scalability + Metrics -->
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] divide-y md:divide-y-0 md:divide-x divide-[#161D30]">
              <div class="px-6 py-5">
                <p class="label mb-3">Scalability</p>
                <p class="font-sans text-[#5A6A88] text-[0.8rem] leading-[1.7] font-light">
                  {project.scalability}
                </p>
              </div>
              <!-- Metrics column -->
              <div class="px-6 py-5 min-w-[160px]">
                <p class="label mb-4">Key Metrics</p>
                <ul class="space-y-4">
                  {project.metrics.map((m) => (
                    <li>
                      <p class="font-mono text-[1.1rem] text-[#10B981] font-semibold leading-none tracking-[-0.02em]">
                        {m.value}
                      </p>
                      <p class="font-mono text-[0.6rem] text-[#3D4E6B] mt-1 tracking-[0.06em]">
                        {m.label}
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Row 5: Tech tags -->
            <div class="px-6 py-4 bg-[#0B0F19]/50 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5 items-center">
              <span class="label mr-2">Stack</span>
              {project.tags.map((tag) => (
                <span class="font-mono text-[0.62rem] text-[#10B981]/65 border border-[#10B981]/12 bg-[#10B981]/[0.03] px-2.5 py-1 rounded-sm tracking-[0.06em] hover:border-[#10B981]/28 hover:text-[#10B981]/90 transition-colors duration-150">
                  {tag}
                </span>
              ))}
            </div>

          </div>

          <!-- Bottom glow on hover -->
          <div class="h-px bg-gradient-to-r from-transparent via-[rgba(16,185,129,0)] to-transparent group-hover:via-[rgba(16,185,129,0.3)] transition-all duration-700"></div>

        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

  </div>
</section>
